/**

@page page_rest_api Colman REST API

@tableofcontents

This page documents the HTTP REST API exposed by the colman server and
consumed by the colman REST client (`RestQueries`).

@section rest_overview Overview

All endpoints are prefixed with `/api`.  Requests and responses use
JSON (`Content-Type: application/json`).  Every request must carry an
`Authorization` header, **except** `GET /api/version` which is public.
See @ref rest_auth for the supported authentication schemes.

@subsection rest_tls Transport Security (HTTPS)

When the server is reachable from outside a private network, running
without TLS is insecure because credentials are transmitted in plain
text.  The server accepts `--cert` and `--key` (PEM files)
to enable HTTPS.

```
colman-rest-server --db library.col --port 443 \
    --cert /etc/ssl/certs/colman.pem \
    --key  /etc/ssl/private/colman.key
```

Both arguments are PEM files.  The private key must be an RSA key.
TLS 1.2 or later is required.

For a publicly reachable server, use a certificate issued by a trusted
CA (e.g. Let's Encrypt via `certbot`).  For a LAN-only server, a
self-signed certificate is sufficient; users must add a browser
exception once.

If neither `--cert` nor `--key` is given, the server starts in plain
HTTP mode and logs a warning.  Plain HTTP is acceptable only for
local-loopback testing.

@subsection rest_db_encryption Database encryption

A `.col` database can be encrypted with SQLite3 Multiple Ciphers.
Encrypted databases protect the data not only against offline theft of the
file (e.g. from a backup), but also against accidentally opening the wrong
collection: a server started with the wrong key or no key at all will refuse
to open the database and exit with an error.

To supply the passphrase to the server without exposing it in the process
list, use one of two methods:

**Key file** (recommended for systemd services):

```
colman-rest-server --db library.col --key-file /path/to/library.key
```

The file must contain the passphrase as plain text.  Store it next to the
database so each instance has its own key file without needing to touch
system directories.  Restrict access so only the service account can read it:

```bash
openssl rand -base64 32 > /var/lib/colman/library.key
chmod 400 /var/lib/colman/library.key
```

**Environment variable** (alternative, e.g. for container deployments):

```bash
export COLMAN_DB_KEY="your-passphrase-here"
colman-rest-server --db library.col
```

`--key-file` takes precedence over `COLMAN_DB_KEY` when both are set.

If neither is provided, the server opens the database without a
passphrase.  Opening an encrypted database without a key fails with
an error at startup.

Versioned resources (Item, Person, Lending, Return, Acquisition,
Liquidation) support optimistic concurrency.  Update requests carry the
expected version in the `If-Match` header; the server responds with
`412 Precondition Failed` when the stored version differs.

HTTP status codes used throughout:

| Code | Meaning                                              |
|------|------------------------------------------------------|
| 200  | OK — request succeeded                               |
| 201  | Created — POST succeeded; body contains `{"id":N}`  |
| 204  | No Content — DELETE succeeded                        |
| 400  | Bad Request — malformed JSON or missing fields       |
| 401  | Unauthorized — missing or invalid bearer token       |
| 404  | Not Found — resource does not exist                  |
| 412  | Precondition Failed — `If-Match` version mismatch   |
| 500  | Internal Server Error                                |

---

@section rest_auth Authentication

Three authentication schemes are supported.  They are evaluated in the
order listed below; the first matching scheme wins.

@subsection rest_auth_basic HTTP Basic Auth — local username/password

```
Authorization: Basic base64(<username>:<password>)
```

The username and Argon2 password hash are stored in the `credentials`
table.  Returns HTTP 401 on wrong credentials.

@subsection rest_auth_barcode HTTP Basic Auth — barcode login (password-less)

```
Authorization: Basic base64(<barcode>:)
```

Enabled only when the server is started with `--allow-barcode-login`.
The username field carries the person's library ID-card barcode; the
password field is left **empty**.

Access is granted only when the person's permission set is restricted to
at most the following three flags: `view_personal_data`, `reserve`,
`view_items`.  Any additional permission causes the attempt to be
rejected with HTTP 401.

This scheme is intended for pupils who use the self-service reservation
feature.

@subsection rest_auth_jwt Bearer JWT (Authentik / OIDC)

```
Authorization: Bearer <jwt>
```

Enabled only when the server is started with `--jwks-uri` and
`--issuer`.  The JWT is an RS256-signed token issued by the configured
OIDC provider.  The `sub` claim is resolved to a colman person via the
`external_id` column in the `credentials` table.  Returns HTTP 401 for
expired or malformed tokens; HTTP 403 when the `sub` is unknown.

---

@section rest_versioning API Versioning

@subsection rest_version_get GET /api/version

Returns the server's REST API version.  **No authentication required.**

Response body:

| Field   | Type   | Description                        |
|---------|--------|------------------------------------|
| `major` | int    | Major version                      |
| `minor` | int    | Minor version                      |
| `name`  | string | Server display name (since 1.1)    |

Both the desktop client and the Flutter client check compatibility before
authenticating, using the rule:
- `server.major == client.major` AND `server.minor >= client.minor`

The desktop client shares `colman_rest_api_version.hpp` with the server; the
Flutter client carries its own copy of the required version in
`flutter/lib/api/api_version.dart`.  An incompatible server is reported to the
user instead of failing later through missing endpoints or fields.

Minor is incremented for backwards-compatible additions; major is
incremented for breaking changes.  The current API version is **2.6**
(see `colman_rest_api_version.hpp`).  Since 2.6, the open copies of an
item held by several persons can be returned in a single request
(@ref rest_returns_batch, group return) and the rows of
`POST /api/persons/lending-query` carry `school_class`.  Since 2.5, several persons can
borrow one copy of an item each in a single request
(@ref rest_lendings_batch, group lending).  Since 2.4, the rows of
`GET /api/lendings/due` carry the borrower's `school_class` separately
(@ref rest_lendings_due).  Since 2.3, lending periods can be
extended (@ref rest_lendings_extend) and `GET /api/history` accepts an
`action` filter (@ref rest_history_get); the Flutter client uses both
and therefore requires minor 3.  Since 2.2, the server generates and
parses barcode format 2 (see @ref sec_barcode_formats); it still parses
legacy format 1 barcodes, and the Flutter client requires minor 2 because it
computes format 2 person barcodes locally.  Since 2.1, `GET /api/table-versions`
also tracks the `types`, `classes` and `collection` tables, so clients can
detect category, class and collection-setting changes (see
@ref rest_table_versions).  Version 2.0 was a breaking change:
card and label layouts are no longer persisted on the server.  Each client
keeps its own card/label layouts locally (clients may drive different
printers).  The former layout endpoints — `PUT /api/collection/id-card-layout`,
`PUT /api/collection/item-card-layout`, `PUT /api/collection/sig-label-layout`,
`PUT /api/collection/id-label-layout-name` and
`PUT /api/collection/item-card-label-layout-name` — were removed, as were the
corresponding `item_card_layout`, `sig_label_layout`, `id_label_layout_name`
and `item_card_label_layout_name` fields of `GET /api/collection`.  The
person `id_card_layout` object is still returned by `GET /api/collection`
read-only, so a freshly installed client can seed its local copy once.  Since
1.20, items can be queried by
acquisition time span (see @ref rest_items_acquired_between).  Since 1.19,
the item-search endpoint accepts the optional `grade_min` / `grade_max`
grade-level span filter (see @ref rest_items_search).  Since 1.14,
person-management
endpoints accept the delegated `create_lenders` / `create_data_collectors`
permissions and enforce the anti-escalation and containment rules described
in @ref rest_persons.  Since 1.13, item types carry a `media_kind` search
category (see @ref rest_types).

@subsection rest_table_versions GET /api/table-versions

Returns per-table change counters used by the REST client to detect
data modifications without re-fetching full result sets.
**Requires authentication.**

Response body (JSON object): one key per tracked table, value is the
current change counter (monotonically increasing integer, starts at 0,
incremented on every INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE in that table):

| Key             | Description                          |
|-----------------|--------------------------------------|
| `items`         | Change counter for the items table   |
| `persons`       | Change counter for persons           |
| `lendings`      | Change counter for lendings          |
| `returns`       | Change counter for returns           |
| `acquisitions`  | Change counter for acquisitions      |
| `liquidations`  | Change counter for liquidations      |
| `reservations`  | Change counter for reservations      |
| `types`         | Change counter for item types (categories) — since 2.1 |
| `classes`       | Change counter for school classes — since 2.1 |
| `collection`    | Change counter for collection settings — since 2.1 |

Both clients poll this endpoint on a configurable interval (default 10 s,
adjustable 5–300 s in the preferences) and refresh only the data the
current page depends on when a *relevant* counter has advanced since the
last poll.  The desktop client scopes the poll to the current page in
`DbChangePoller` and calls the appropriate `refresh_*_pages()` function; the
Flutter client uses the `tableVersionsProvider` poller together with the
`watchTables()` helper, by which each screen reloads itself when the tables
it shows change.  When a poll fails to reach the server, both clients flag
the data as possibly stale (a warning dialog on the desktop, a persistent
banner in the Flutter client).

@subsection rest_me GET /api/me

Returns identity and permission flags for the authenticated caller.
Requires permission `reserve`.

Response body:

| Field              | Type | Description                                        |
|--------------------|------|----------------------------------------------------|
| `person_id`        | int  | Person ID of the caller (0 for the admin account) |
| `edit_reservations`| bool | Whether the caller has the `edit_reservations` permission |

This endpoint is used by clients to determine whether to show the
full reservation management UI (person search, all reservations) or the
self-service view (own reservations only).

---

@section rest_collection Collection

@subsection rest_collection_get GET /api/collection

Returns the collection metadata.

**Response body** (JSON object):

| Field             | Type   | Description                       |
|-------------------|--------|-----------------------------------|
| `name`            | string | Collection name                   |
| `prefix`          | string | Barcode prefix                    |
| `school`          | string | School name                       |
| `address`         | string | School address                    |
| `num_items_digits`| int    | Number of digits in item barcodes |

@subsection rest_collection_put PUT /api/collection

Saves collection metadata.  Request body has the same shape as the
GET response.

---

@section rest_types Item Types

Localized display names (`locale`, attribute labels) are returned in the
language requested by the `Accept-Language` header (two-letter code, e.g.
`de` or `en`); without the header, the server's system language is used.
The predefined types carry German and English names.  The desktop REST
client sends the language of its user interface, i.e. the first
`LANGUAGE`/`LANG` entry for which colman has a translation, else `en`.

@subsection rest_types_list GET /api/types

Returns a JSON array of all item types:

| Field        | Type   | Description                  |
|--------------|--------|------------------------------|
| `id`         | int    | Type ID                      |
| `name`       | string | Internal name                |
| `locale`     | string | Localized display name       |
| `media_kind` | int    | Search category (see below)  |
| `attributes` | object | Map of attribute key → label |

`media_kind` maps the type to an internet search category, independently
of its (possibly localized) name: 0 = none, 1 = book, 2 = CD, 3 = DVD,
4 = Blu-ray, 5 = board game.  It drives which title/barcode search
services a client offers for items of this type.  Added in API 1.13;
absent or 0 means "no search category".

@subsection rest_types_get GET /api/types/{id}

Returns the type with the given ID, including its `media_kind`.

@subsection rest_types_byname GET /api/types/by-name/{name}

Returns the type with the given internal name.

@subsection rest_types_locale GET /api/types/locale

Returns a JSON array of `{"id": N, "locale": "..."}` pairs for all
types.

@subsection rest_types_names GET /api/types/names

Returns a JSON array of `{"id": N, "name": "..."}` pairs for all
types.

@subsection rest_types_post POST /api/types

Creates a new item type.  Request body:

| Field        | Type   | Required | Description                       |
|--------------|--------|----------|-----------------------------------|
| `name`       | string | yes      | Internal name                     |
| `locale`     | string | yes      | Localized label                   |
| `media_kind` | int    | no       | Search category (default 0, none) |

Response body: `{"id": N}`.

@subsection rest_types_put PUT /api/types/{id}

Updates an existing type.  Accepts the same `media_kind` field as
@ref rest_types_post.

@subsection rest_types_delete DELETE /api/types/{id}

Deletes the type.

@subsection rest_types_attributes GET /api/types/{id}/attributes

Returns a JSON object mapping attribute key → localized label for the
given type.

@subsection rest_types_attr_set PUT /api/types/{id}/attributes

Saves the attribute map.  Request body: JSON object of key → label.

@subsection rest_types_thumbnail GET /api/types/{id}/thumbnail

Returns the thumbnail for items of the given type.  Response is a JSON
array of objects:

| Field       | Type   | Description       |
|-------------|--------|-------------------|
| `item_id`   | int    | Item ID           |
| `item_name` | string | Item name         |
| `type_name` | string | Type name         |
| `thumb_id`  | int    | Thumbnail blob ID |

@subsection rest_types_restrict POST /api/types/{id}/restrict

Restricts an item type to a given item.

---

@section rest_signatures Signature Labels

@subsection rest_sig_top GET /api/signatures/top

Returns the current top-line signature template as a JSON object.

@subsection rest_sig_top_put PUT /api/signatures/top

Saves the top-line signature template.

@subsection rest_sig_type GET /api/signatures/top/{type_id}

Returns the signature template for the given type.

@subsection rest_sig_type_put PUT /api/signatures/top/{type_id}

Saves the signature template for the given type.

@subsection rest_sig_type_delete DELETE /api/signatures/top/{type_id}

Deletes the per-type signature template (falls back to default).

@subsection rest_sig_type_name GET /api/signatures/top/by-name/{type_name}

Returns the signature template for the type identified by its internal
name.

@subsection rest_sig_bottom_all GET /api/signatures/bottom

Returns a JSON array with the names of all bottom-line signature
categories, regardless of their associated top category.  Use
@c GET /api/signatures/top/{top}/bottom to filter by a specific top
category instead.

---

@section rest_items Items

@subsection rest_items_list GET /api/items?id={id}&id={id}...

Returns a JSON array of item-list rows for the given item IDs (query
parameters may be repeated).  Each element:

| Field             | Type   | Description               |
|-------------------|--------|---------------------------|
| `id`              | int    | Item ID                   |
| `name`            | string | Item name                 |
| `count`           | int    | Total stock count         |
| `available`       | int    | Currently available count |
| `type_translated` | string | Localized type label      |
| `type_name`       | string | Internal type name        |
| `collector`       | string | Data-collector name       |

@subsection rest_items_get GET /api/items/{id}

Returns the full `Item` object as a JSON object.  See
@ref rest_item_json for the schema.

@subsection rest_items_post POST /api/items

Creates a new item.  Request body: `Item` JSON.  Response: `{"id":N}`.

The `academic_year` field is an array of `[first, second]` grade-level
ranges.  Each range must be well-formed (`first <= second`); otherwise the
server responds with `400 Bad Request` and
`{"error": "Invalid academic year range"}`.

@subsection rest_items_put PUT /api/items/{id}

Updates an item.  Requires `If-Match: {version}`.  The same
`academic_year` range validation as for @ref rest_items_post applies.

@subsection rest_items_delete DELETE /api/items/{id}

Deletes the item.

@subsection rest_items_count GET /api/items/{id}/count

Returns `{"value": N}` with the stock count.

@subsection rest_items_available GET /api/items/available?id={id}&search={s}&limit={n}&offset={n}

Returns a JSON array of item-list rows for available items (same shape
as `/api/items`).  Optional query parameters:
- `id=N` — filter by item ID
- `search=<string>` — filter by search string
- `limit=N` — maximum number of rows to return (0 = unlimited)
- `offset=N` — skip the first N matching rows (pagination)

@subsection rest_items_acquired GET /api/items/acquired?id={id}&search={s}&limit={n}&offset={n}

Returns a JSON array of item-list rows for acquired items (same shape
as `/api/items`).  Optional query parameters:
- `id=N` — filter by item ID
- `search=<string>` — filter by search string
- `limit=N` — maximum number of rows to return (0 = unlimited)
- `offset=N` — skip the first N matching rows (pagination)

@subsection rest_items_recently_acquired GET /api/items/recently-acquired?count={n}

Returns a JSON array of `{"id": N}` objects for the most recently acquired
items, ordered newest acquisition first.  Items without any acquisition
record are omitted.
- `count=N` — maximum number of item ids to return

@subsection rest_items_acquired_between GET /api/items/acquired-between?from={iso}&to={iso}

Returns a JSON array of `{"id": N}` objects for the items acquired within
the inclusive time span `[from, to]`, ordered by id.  Items without any
matching acquisition record are omitted.  Added in API version 1.20.
- `from=ISO` — start of the time span (inclusive), ISO 8601 date/time
- `to=ISO` — end of the time span (inclusive), ISO 8601 date/time

@subsection rest_items_byisbn GET /api/items/by-isbn/{isbn}

Returns the full `Item` object matching the given ISBN, or 404.

@subsection rest_items_byean GET /api/items/by-ean/{ean}

Returns the full `Item` object matching the given EAN, or 404.

@subsection rest_items_barcode_gen GET /api/items/barcode?type_id={id}&count={N}

Returns `{"value": "..."}` containing a newly generated barcode string
for the given type.

@subsection rest_items_barcode_parse POST /api/items/barcode/parse

Parses a barcode string.  Request body: `{"barcode":"..."}`.  Response:
`{"id":N}` (the item ID encoded in the barcode).

@subsection rest_items_barcode_check GET /api/items/barcode/check?barcode={b}

Returns `{"value": N}` where N is the item ID if the barcode is valid,
or -1.

@subsection rest_items_lentquery POST /api/items/lent-query

Returns the lent count for a given item/lending pair.  Request body:
`{"item_id":N, "lending_id":M}`.  Response: `{"value":K}`.

@subsection rest_items_withtype GET /api/items/with-type?type_id={id}&search={s}&limit={n}&offset={n}

Returns a JSON array of item-list rows for items of the given type.
Optional query parameters:
- `search=<string>` — filter by search string
- `limit=N` — maximum number of rows to return (0 = unlimited)
- `offset=N` — skip the first N matching rows (pagination)

@subsection rest_items_search POST /api/items/search

Searches items.  Request body:

| Field         | Type   | Description                           |
|---------------|--------|---------------------------------------|
| `general`     | string | Full-text search across all fields    |
| `name`        | string | Filter by item name                   |
| `subject`     | string | Filter by subject field               |
| `description` | string | Filter by description field           |
| `keywords`    | string | Filter by keywords                    |
| `type_id`     | int    | Filter by type (-1 for all)           |
| `type_fields` | array  | Per-type attribute filters (see below)|
| `grade_min`   | int    | Lowest wanted grade level (0 = none)  |
| `grade_max`   | int    | Highest wanted grade level (0 = none) |
| `limit`       | int    | Maximum number of rows (0 = unlimited)|
| `offset`      | int    | Pagination offset                     |

Each element of `type_fields` is an object with `column` (string) and
`value` (string) fields.

`grade_min` / `grade_max` (since 1.19) restrict school items to those
whose stored grade-level range overlaps the span `[grade_min, grade_max]`.
A bound of 0 leaves that side open.  When either bound is set, items
without a grade level are excluded from the results.

Response: JSON array of search-result rows:

| Field         | Type   | Description               |
|---------------|--------|---------------------------|
| `id`          | int    | Item ID                   |
| `name`        | string | Item name                 |
| `type_locale` | string | Localized type label      |
| `count`       | int    | Stock count               |
| `available`   | int    | Available count           |
| `type_id`     | int    | Type ID                   |

@subsection rest_items_completions GET /api/items/completions/{prefix}

Returns a JSON array of name-completion strings for items whose name
starts with `prefix`.

@subsection rest_items_school_completions GET /api/items/school-completions/{prefix}

Returns school-level completions for the given prefix.

@subsection rest_items_thumbnail GET /api/items/{id}/thumbnail

Returns the item thumbnail as PNG bytes (`Content-Type: image/png`),
or 404.

@subsection rest_items_thumbnail_put PUT /api/items/{id}/thumbnail

Stores a thumbnail.  Request body: raw PNG bytes
(`Content-Type: image/png`).

---

@section rest_persons Persons

@par Person-management authorization

Creating, editing or deleting persons and changing their permissions,
credentials, username, class or external ID requires one of the
person-management permissions: `edit_persons`, `create_lenders` or
`create_data_collectors`.  Two rules are enforced on every such request:

- <b>Anti-escalation:</b> a caller may only grant a permission they hold
  themselves (the administrator holds all).  `edit_persons` is no exception.
- <b>Containment:</b> a caller may only manage a target whose current
  permission set lies within their own scope — `edit_persons` grants any
  target whose rights are a subset of the caller's; `create_lenders` only
  borrower-profile targets (`view_items`, `reserve`); `create_data_collectors`
  only data-collector-profile targets (`collect_acquisitions`,
  `collect_liquidations`, `view_items`).

Requests that violate either rule return `403 Forbidden`.

@subsection rest_persons_list GET /api/persons?id={id}&id={id}...

Returns a JSON array of person-list rows.  Each element:

| Field       | Type   | Description            |
|-------------|--------|------------------------|
| `id`        | int    | Person ID              |
| `lastname`  | string | Last name              |
| `firstname` | string | First name             |
| `title`     | string | Title / class          |
| `has_password` | bool | Password is set     |

@subsection rest_persons_get GET /api/persons/{id}

Returns the full `Person` object.  See @ref rest_person_json.
Requires permission `view_persons` unless the caller requests their
own record (since 1.11).

@subsection rest_persons_post POST /api/persons

Creates a new person.  Request body: `Person` JSON.  Response:
`{"id":N}`.

@subsection rest_persons_put PUT /api/persons/{id}

Updates a person.  Requires `If-Match: {version}`.

@subsection rest_persons_delete DELETE /api/persons/{id}

Deletes the person.

@subsection rest_persons_startpage GET /api/persons/start-page?search={s}

Returns a JSON array of start-page person rows matching the search
string.

@subsection rest_persons_bybarcode GET /api/persons/by-barcode/{barcode}

Returns the full `Person` object matching the barcode.

@subsection rest_persons_known GET /api/persons/known?lastname={l}&firstname={f}&title={t}

Returns `{"value":N}` with the person ID if a person with the given
name is known, else -1.

@subsection rest_persons_recently_created GET /api/persons/recently-created?count={n}

Returns a JSON array of `{"id": N}` objects for the most recently created
persons, ordered newest first.  As the persons table carries no creation
timestamp, the person id is used as the creation-order proxy; the
Administrator (id 0) is excluded.
- `count=N` — maximum number of person ids to return

@subsection rest_persons_byextid GET /api/persons/by-external-id/{external_id}

Returns the full `Person` matching the external ID, or 404.

@subsection rest_persons_completions GET /api/persons/completions/{prefix}

Returns a JSON array of name-completion strings.

@subsection rest_persons_extid_set PUT /api/persons/{id}/external-id

Links the person to an OIDC identity.
Request body: `{"sub":"<subject>"}`.

@subsection rest_persons_barcode GET /api/persons/{id}/barcode

Returns `{"value": "..."}` containing the person's Code128 barcode
string (collection prefix, person marker, ID, check digit).  Requires
the `view_persons` permission.

@subsection rest_permissions_list GET /api/permissions

Returns a JSON array of permission column name strings (the canonical
list used as index positions in per-person permission arrays).

@subsection rest_persons_permissions_get GET /api/persons/{id}/permissions

Returns a JSON array of booleans, one per permission column, in the
same order as `GET /api/permissions`.

@subsection rest_persons_permissions_defaults POST /api/persons/{id}/permissions/defaults

Inserts a default (all-false) permission row for the person.

@subsection rest_persons_permissions_set PUT /api/persons/{id}/permissions/{column}

Sets a single permission column.
Request body: `{"value":true}` or `{"value":false}`.

@subsection rest_persons_permissions_delete DELETE /api/persons/{id}/permissions

Removes all permission entries for the person.

@subsection rest_persons_password_set POST /api/persons/{id}/password

Sets the person's REST login credentials (username + password hash).
Request body: `{"username":"<name>","password":"<argon2-hash>"}`.
Both fields are required; returns HTTP 400 if either is missing.

@subsection rest_persons_password_delete DELETE /api/persons/{id}/password

Removes the person's password hash only.  The username entry is kept.

@subsection rest_persons_username_get GET /api/persons/{id}/username

Returns the person's username: `{"username":"<name>"}` or
`{"username":null}` when no username has been assigned.

@subsection rest_persons_username_set PUT /api/persons/{id}/username

Assigns or changes the person's username.
Request body: `{"username":"<name>"}`.
Returns HTTP 409 Conflict if the name is already taken.

@subsection rest_persons_username_delete DELETE /api/persons/{id}/username

Clears the person's username (sets it to NULL).  Returns HTTP 204.

@subsection rest_persons_change_passwords GET /api/persons/change-passwords

Returns a JSON array of all persons (including the Administrator) for
the password-management page.  Each element:

| Field       | Type   | Description                          |
|-------------|--------|--------------------------------------|
| `id`        | int    | Person ID (0 = Administrator)        |
| `lastname`  | string | Last name                            |
| `firstname` | string | First name                           |
| `title`     | string | Title                                |
| `username`  | string | Username, or empty string if not set |

@subsection rest_persons_class_get GET /api/persons/{id}/class

Returns `{"value":"..."}` with the person's class string.

@subsection rest_persons_class_set PUT /api/persons/{id}/class

Sets the class.  Request body: `{"value":"..."}`.

@subsection rest_persons_thumbnail GET /api/persons/{id}/thumbnail

Returns the person photo as PNG bytes, or 404.

@subsection rest_persons_thumbnail_put PUT /api/persons/{id}/thumbnail

Stores a person photo.  Request body: raw PNG bytes.

---

@section rest_lendings Lendings

@subsection rest_lendings_list GET /api/lendings?id={id}&id={id}...

Returns a JSON array of open-lending rows.  Each element:

| Field            | Type   | Description               |
|------------------|--------|---------------------------|
| `id`             | int    | Lending ID                |
| `lent_count`     | int    | Number lent               |
| `returned_count` | int    | Number returned so far    |
| `datetime`       | string | ISO 8601 lending datetime |
| `duedate`        | string | ISO 8601 due date         |
| `remarks`        | string | Remarks                   |
| `person_name`    | string | Borrower display name     |

@subsection rest_lendings_get GET /api/lendings/{id}

Returns the full `Lending` object.

@subsection rest_lendings_post POST /api/lendings

Creates a new lending.  Request body: `Lending` JSON.  Response:
`{"id":N}`.

If copies are blocked by another user's reservation, returns HTTP 409
with `{"error":"reserved","not_reserved_count":N}` where `N` is the
number of unreserved copies that could still be lent.

If no (or not enough) copies are available — e.g. the item has meanwhile
been lent out by someone else — returns HTTP 409 with
`{"error":"not_available","available_count":N}` where `N` is the number of
copies still available.  The server never reports an unavailable lending as
success.  `POST /api/returns`, `/api/acquisitions` and `/api/liquidations`
likewise return HTTP 409 when the requested operation is rejected (e.g.
returning more copies than are outstanding).

@subsection rest_lendings_put PUT /api/lendings/{id}

Updates a lending.  Requires `If-Match: {version}`.

@subsection rest_lendings_delete DELETE /api/lendings/{id}

Deletes the lending.

@subsection rest_lendings_balance GET /api/lendings/{id}/balance

Returns `{"value":N}` with the outstanding (lent - returned) count.

@subsection rest_lendings_person GET /api/lendings/person/{person_id}

Returns a JSON array of `LendingPersonInfo` objects for the given
person:

| Field               | Type   | Description            |
|---------------------|--------|------------------------|
| `lending_id`        | int    | Lending ID             |
| `person_last_name`  | string | Person last name       |
| `person_first_name` | string | Person first name      |
| `person_title`      | string | Person title / class   |

@subsection rest_lendings_info GET /api/lendings/{id}/info

Returns a `LendingInfo` object:

| Field           | Type   | Description      |
|-----------------|--------|------------------|
| `lending_id`    | int    | Lending ID       |
| `lending_count` | int    | Lent count       |
| `item_name`     | string | Item name        |

@subsection rest_lendings_due GET /api/lendings/due?from={date}

Returns a JSON array of due-lending rows for lendings due on or before
`from` (ISO 8601 date).  Allowed with `view_history`, `collect_returns`
or `collect_lendings` (needed to extend a person's lendings), or for the
caller's own lendings (`person_id` = own id):

| Field              | Type   | Description            |
|--------------------|--------|------------------------|
| `lending_id`       | int    | Lending ID             |
| `item_id`          | int    | Item ID                |
| `person_id`        | int    | Person ID              |
| `duedate`          | string | Due date               |
| `person_name`      | string | Borrower display name: "lastname, firstname", followed by " (class)" if the borrower has a school class |
| `school_class`     | string | Borrower's school class, empty if none (since 2.4) |
| `outstanding_count`| int    | Unreturned count       |
| `item_name`        | string | Item name              |

`person_name` keeps the class suffix for clients older than 2.4; the
desktop client strips it and shows `school_class` in a column of its own.

@subsection rest_lendings_extend POST /api/lendings/extend

Sets a new due date for several lendings at once ("extend lending
period").  Requires `collect_lendings`.  Since API 2.3.

Request body:

| Field         | Type      | Description                              |
|---------------|-----------|------------------------------------------|
| `lending_ids` | int array | Lendings to change (must not be empty)   |
| `duedate`     | string    | New due date (ISO 8601); empty = none    |

All lendings are changed in one transaction and their version is
incremented.  Response: `204 No Content`; `400 Bad Request` for an empty
id list; `404 Not Found` if a lending does not exist (nothing is changed).

@subsection rest_lendings_batch POST /api/lendings/batch

Group lending: lends one copy of an item to each of several persons.
Requires `collect_lendings`.  Since API 2.5.

Request body:

| Field        | Type      | Description                                   |
|--------------|-----------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `lending`    | object    | `Lending` template: item, datetime, duedate, remarks, data collector; person and count are ignored |
| `person_ids` | int array | Borrowers (must not be empty)                 |

All lendings are inserted in one transaction -- either every person
gets a lending with count 1 or nothing is changed.  A borrower's own
reservation of the item is consumed like with @ref rest_lendings_post.
Response: `{"ids":[N,...]}` with the new lending ids in the order of
`person_ids`.

Errors:
- `400 Bad Request` for a missing template or an empty person list.
- `409 Conflict` with `{"error":"not_available","available_count":N}`
  if the copies do not suffice; `N` is the number of copies that can
  be lent to this group (available copies minus those still reserved
  for others).
- `409 Conflict` with `{"error":"already_lent","person_id":P}` if
  person `P` still holds an open lending of the item.

---

@section rest_returns Returns

@subsection rest_returns_post POST /api/returns

Creates a new return.  Request body: `Return` JSON.  Response:
`{"id":N}`.

@subsection rest_returns_batch POST /api/returns/batch

Group return: returns all copies of an item still on loan to several
persons.  Requires `collect_returns`.  Since API 2.6.

Request body:

| Field        | Type      | Description                                   |
|--------------|-----------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `item_id`    | int       | The item                                      |
| `person_ids` | int array | Returning persons (must not be empty)         |
| `return`     | object    | `Return` template: date_time, remarks, data collector; lending_id and count are ignored |

All returns are inserted in one transaction: one return per open
lending of the item held by one of the persons, covering its remaining
count -- or nothing is changed.  Response: `{"ids":[N,...],"count":C}`
with the new return ids and the total number of returned copies.

Errors:
- `400 Bad Request` for a missing template, item or person list.
- `409 Conflict` with `{"error":"not_lent","person_id":P}` if person
  `P` holds no open lending of the item.

@subsection rest_returns_get GET /api/returns/{id}

Returns the full `Return` object.

@subsection rest_returns_put PUT /api/returns/{id}

Updates a return.  Requires `If-Match: {version}`.

@subsection rest_returns_delete DELETE /api/returns/{id}

Deletes the return.

---

